WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.400
A power monitor faces a dilemma.

2
00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:07.980
Ignore the constant ripple from a converter yet still catch the brownout

3
00:00:07.980 --> 00:00:09.640
that would corrupt memory.

4
00:00:09.640 --> 00:00:12.798
Each rail is watched through its own precision divider,

5
00:00:12.798 --> 00:00:16.478
so the threshold barely moves when the temperature does.

6
00:00:16.486 --> 00:00:20.286
A fault has to last longer than 8 microseconds to count.

7
00:00:20.746 --> 00:00:23.486
Converter ripple dies inside that window.

8
00:00:23.986 --> 00:00:26.246
A real brownout survives it.

9
00:00:26.246 --> 00:00:29.724
The fault is latched and recorded, the warning pin drops first,

10
00:00:29.724 --> 00:00:31.504
and the reset follows.

11
00:00:31.804 --> 00:00:33.964
A fault cannot pass unnoticed.

12
00:00:34.023 --> 00:00:38.923
One trimmed reference sets every threshold, adjusted once at test to a

13
00:00:38.923 --> 00:00:40.023
fraction of a percent.

14
00:00:40.360 --> 00:00:43.560
The watchdog guards the processor, not the rails.

15
00:00:44.080 --> 00:00:46.440
A kick too early, or never at all,

16
00:00:46.440 --> 00:00:48.600
and the processor restarts.

17
00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:50.419
SKU-6 is pre-silicon.

18
00:00:50.419 --> 00:00:54.159
None of these thresholds has been measured on silicon yet

19
00:00:55.119 --> 00:00:57.919
Bring us your rails and your brownout budget.

20
00:00:57.919 --> 00:01:01.059
We will tell you what SKU-6 must prove.
